{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 11, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that delves deep into the origins and controversies surrounding facebook. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the movie brilliantly captures the cutthroat world of silicon valley and the birth of a digital empire.\n\njesse eisenberg delivers a standout performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying the character as both brilliant and socially awkward. his portrayal is nuanced, making mark’s evolution from a vengeful harvard student to a billionaire entrepreneur both compelling and unsettling. andrew garfield shines as eduardo savarin, the loyal best friend whose betrayal by mark serves as the emotional core of the story. justin timberlake also gives a memorable performance as sean parker, the enigmatic and cunning co-founder of napster who becomes a divisive figure in facebook’s early days.\n\nthe film’s screenplay is sharp, fast-paced, and packed with sharp dialogue that sorkin is known for. the non-linear storytelling effectively juxtaposes the legal battles and personal fallouts against the rapid rise of facebook, creating a gripping narrative. the cinematography by jeff cronenweth is sleek and stylish, with a color palette that reflects the cold, digital world of the early 2000s. the score by trent reznor and atticus ross adds a haunting, electronic edge that perfectly complements the film’s themes of ambition, betrayal, and isolation.\n\none of the film’s greatest strengths is its ability to humanize its characters while also exposing their flaws. mark zuckerberg is portrayed as a genius but also as morally ambiguous, making him a fascinating protagonist. the supporting cast, including armie hammer as twins cameron and tyler winklevoss, brenda song as christy lee, and max minghella as divya narendra, adds depth and humor to the story.\n\nthe social network isn’t just a film 